  "subject": "🧳 Axios Finish Line: Toddler tour guide",
  "date": "Thu, 29 Oct 2026 21:29:30 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🥛 Plus: Kindness in the dairy aisle | Thursday, October 29, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Oct 29, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Axios' Carly Mallenbaum is hosting tonight, sharing lessons learned from traveling to Europe with her son.\n\n Smart Brevity™ count: 514 words … 2 mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Travel like a toddler \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n The writer and her son admire \"David\" by Gian Lorenzo Bernini at the Borghese Gallery. Photo: Carly Mallenbaum/Axios Carly writes: \n\n Because we weren't going to change who we were once we had kids, my travel-loving husband and I insisted on bringing our toddler on a very adult trip to Portugal and Italy this summer.\n\n Think: museum visits, Airbnbs, history tours and restaurant reservations. Why it matters: Of course, traveling with a toddler changed the pace of the trip.\n\n It also changed what I considered worth noticing. 🏟️ Zoom in: We splurged on a private Colosseum tour in Rome, but I couldn't even tell you when the arena was constructed. I was too busy looking at holes in the ancient stones with my 2-year-old.\n\n I have not a single fact to share from the Doge's Palace in Venice, which we left quickly because I sensed a tantrum percolating. I can, however, describe the choreography of how suitcases go from conveyor belt to truck to plane at the Venice airport. Traveling with my toddler redirected my attention.\n\n I'm usually the tourist reading every museum plaque, absorbing the history, taking notes and trying to fit in. This time, I followed my son's lead. 🖼️ At the Borghese Gallery in Rome, my son learned that announcing \"Bernini! Bernini! Bernini!\" in a very quiet museum made even serious tourists giggle. And I didn't rush to quiet him.\n\n In a piazza, we kicked a soccer ball with Italian kids, and I didn't even think to stop for a photo. At a bar in Lisbon, he sat transfixed by a fado performance, and I didn't immediately reach for a translation or to write in my journal. ✈️ The big picture: We aren't the only parents naive or ambitious enough to travel with a little one.\n\n A Tripadvisor survey from 2024 found that 81% of new parents planned on traveling with their child. According to the travel site's 2026 trend report , experience bookings containing children's tickets increased 19% year over year. \"People don't want to just do water slides and water parks,\" says Melissa Zimmerman, my friend and the founder of And We're Off travel agency. \"They want to bring their kids along for the adventure.\"\n\n Making that work, she says, often comes down to choosing the right home base, scheduling around naps and leaving room for downtime. 🚤 The bottom line: My son didn't become our kind of tourist. I became his.\n\n When I picture Venice now, I don't first see St. Mark's Square or the Rialto Bridge. I think of my son beside the canal, cataloging the boats: police boat, trash boat, \"red one.\" Share this story . \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n See what's next for media in 2027 \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive Annual members receive the 2026 Annual Report, our special media document featuring global forecasts, sector analysis and exclusive media data insights.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n🔒 Get the report by becoming a Media Trends Executive member.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 👓 Stranger&#x27;s kindness \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Allie Carl/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Today's random good deed, from reader Amelia Gaillard of Jacksonville, Fla.:\n\n \"Having forgotten my readers in the car, I was squinting at labels in the grocery store dairy aisle when the stranger next to me offered his glasses without a word. I've remembered his small gesture as a large kindness. He noticed a need. He helped.\" \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
